hey guys welcome to the behind the  scenes of wizard barf the movie  you know when they spent all that time  at nasa researching fluid simulations  and dynamics  it was all so that we could do  potion puke coming out of wizard's  mouths  so in coming up with this idea we play a  lot of dungeons and dragons and when we  play dungeons and dragons we like to  make up  crazy potions we're thinking hey what  would happen if we made a video about  wizards who had too many potions they  puke a lot and just be vomit all over  the place yeah so we decided to define  that for the internet so in order to do  the vomit simulations we use a program  called realflow realflow is basically  known for doing really good fluid  simulations they use it all the time in  commercials it's first we film our shot  with us pretending puke without anything  there so it kind of looked like  goofballs just in an alleyway dressing  up like wizards in the middle of like  this alley in los angeles and every once  in a while people would stumble into the  alley and like peek in and there'd be  like one wizard with a beard and all  this messed up face filming this other  wizard with a beard with another messed  up face and we just like turn to them  with their hoods but they just see two  wizards filming each other like coughing  in an alley yeah just pretending to  throw up probably was the most bizarre  scene for most people that day  one of the things you can do to like  take your effects to the next level is  do 3d camera tracking yeah we've talked  about it a lot before and we're planning  still  to do a tutorial about it yeah sorry  i've done the chance basically we use a  motion tracking program there's a lot of  different kinds out there to track the  position of the camera then we rebuilt  some of the geometry of the scene so  that the puke could interact with it for  example you'll see it bouncing off the  walls and hitting the ground and all  that kind of stuff so we need to get the  bark to come out of our mouths once  again in this case it was easiest for us  to just animate it by hand so you want  to come up with like a system that makes  it quick and easy so the first thing i  did is i would just take an object in  this case cylinder and just kind of  track it to the position of the person  roughly like every five frames move it  to the new spot then i put a little tiny  pyramid  where my mouth was pointing in the  direction where the barf would come out  i would parent the pyramid to the to the  cylinder or whatever object was  representing the person so as the person  moved this little like emitter would  roughly be in the same spot then all i  had to do was go around and make little  tweaks to actually kind of keep it on  the map so anyways after doing this for  every shot  we could then take this into the  program for fluid simulations called  realflow so once we built the scene and  animated where the guys have to barf in  realflow uh there's two stages to making  your liquid the first is basically  having a particle pass where you spew  out all these little particles that have  the properties of water such as like  surface tension viscosity you know they  glob together and they're running and  stuff like that  but they're not actually liquid yet  once you have that first particle  simulation done you actually make a mesh  out of it which is the program looks at  all the dots and where they are and  makes an averaged out 3d model that  coats and surrounds all the particles  giving it that actual solid liquid form  and once we have that we basically bring  that right back into 3ds max and it is  just a model an animated moving  displacing model so anyways we bring  this back into 3ds max and you have  geometry that now represents the liquid  but now you got to make the liquid look  like liquid so we do all our rendering  in a renderer called mental ray it comes  with 3ds max  and it's really really good at doing  accurate light simulation and really  good materials and surface properties on  set for the stuff we were drinking it's  all just like gatorade and powerade with  maybe a little extra food coloring to  make it a little more vibrant so that's  kind of like what we tried to replicate  so in 3ds max i made a material that was  the correct amount of shininess correct  amount of transparency had a little bit  of translucency a little bit that milky  kind of like quality that the potions  had that we were drinking as long as  you've set up your lights correctly in  the scene once you build all that  things actually end up looking pretty  realistic when you put it all together  so you just render out passes you render  out your puke pass without any shadows  then you write out shadow paths bring  that into after effects everything is  motion tracked so you just simply drop  it in drop in your shadows do a little  bit tweaking of the levels always tweak  the levels  and then you have your final shot yo  extra special pro tip real smart motion  blur it calculates motion blur  on anything so rather than having to  deal with trying to figure out how to  render out motion blur for this you just  apply real smart motion blur now for the  fire we used something called fume  effects which is a plug-in for 3ds max  it specializes in creating smoke and  fire now as you can see here i had to go  through a lot of iterations to get what  i wanted and it looks really really bad  but that's because i'm simulating in low  resolution with low quality because it  goes much much faster and i can hone in  on the motion and movement that i want  once i have that i then simulate a high  resolution version which takes much  longer but the flames look much more  detailed i take this into after effects  play with the colors to make them more  match the scene add some embers some  glow and some motion blur to make it all  look more realistic and tie it together  and then i have my final composition  so this is the outro where